// The `arc` ownership mode.
//
// Status: parses, passes `cell check`, and the emitted C compiles, links and
// runs. It prints 13, and that number is a refcount measurement rather than
// arithmetic: `observe` reports the strong count it was handed, so the two
// 3s below are the retains this file's own call sites inserted. See
// examples/arc_host.c for the two bodyless declarations' C side, and
// tools/check.sh's execution section for the command that runs it.
//
// The C backend implements the retain half of docs/OWNERSHIP.md R11:
// a literal bound as `arc` is boxed with cell_arc_from_string, an `arc` place
// passed to an `arc` parameter is cloned at the call site, and an `arc` place
// passed to a `shared` parameter is deliberately NOT cloned (R8 makes the
// borrow safe). The release half is the drop pass, which emits cell_arc_drop
// at scope exit and before every return.
//
// The runtime machinery is real: cell_arc_new, cell_arc_clone, and
// cell_arc_drop in runtime/cell_rt.c do genuine refcount increments and
// decrements and call a drop function at zero. As of 562f116 those refcounts
// are atomic (`_Atomic` in the .c file).
//
// The LLVM and MLIR backends still refuse this file, deliberately and
// identically: they are scalar-first and emit no drops at all.

pub struct Session {
    arc name: String
    copy id: Int
}

pub fn print_int(copy value: Int);

/// An `arc` argument is never made dead by passing it. Both the caller and the
/// callee hold a live reference afterward. That is the whole point of the mode.
/// The C side returns the strong count it observed, so the printed total below
/// is evidence about retains rather than a hand-written constant.
pub fn observe(arc name: String) -> Int;

/// A returned `arc` is returned already retained: the caller owns that
/// reference and must release it. See docs/OWNERSHIP.md R11.
///
/// This body returns its own parameter, so the caller's call-site retain is
/// the reference handed back and nothing extra is emitted here. R11 rule 3
/// holds by construction: a parameter is never dropped.
pub fn share_name(arc name: String) -> arc String {
    return name
}

/// Passing an `arc` place to a `shared` parameter borrows the pointee for the
/// call and does NOT retain. Safe because a borrow may not escape (R8).
/// The C side returns the borrowed view's length, which is 7.
pub fn inspect(shared name: String) -> Int;

pub fn main() {
    let arc label = "session"
    let arc alias = share_name(arc label)
    let copy first = observe(arc label)
    let copy second = observe(arc alias)
    let copy third = inspect(shared label)
    print_int(first + second + third)
}

// `arc` grants shared access only in this revision. Mutating through `arc` is
// an error (OWNERSHIP.md R9), because Cell has no interior mutability yet.
// See examples/rejected/arc_mutation.cell.
//
// `Session` is declared and not constructed on purpose. Its `arc name` field
// shows the struct lowering (`cell_arc_t name;`), but a struct is not dropped
// by this backend at all, so building one here would leak the field's retain
// and spoil the refcount arithmetic this file is measuring. The struct-field
// retain of R11 rule 4 is covered by a codegen test instead.
